  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "To estimate the probability that a randomly selected driver drives the SUV or received a speeding ticket but not both, we\n\n- add up the numbers in the row **\"Speeding ticket\"** or the column **\"SUV\"**, but not in both, and then\n\n- divide by the total number of data points in the table.\n\n|  | SUV | Sport car | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Speeding ticket | $80$ | $5$ | $85$ |\n| No speeding ticket | $20$ | $5$ | $25$ |\n| Totals | $100$ | $10$ | $110$ |\n\nAdding up these numbers and then dividing by the total number of data points in the table $(110),$ we get\n\n$$\np=\\frac{20+5}{110}=\\frac{5}{22}.\n$$\n\nThis represents the estimated probability that a randomly selected driver drives an SUV or received a speeding ticket but not both.\n\nFinally, to find the estimated number of drivers in the second company that drive SUVs or received a speeding ticket, but not both, we multiply the total number of drivers in the company $(220)$ by the probability above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =220\\cdot p \\\\  & =220\\cdot \\frac{5}{22} \\\\  & =50\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
